The Nautilus DevOps team is strategically planning the migration of a portion of their infrastructure to the AWS cloud. 
Acknowledging the magnitude of this endeavor, they have chosen to tackle the migration incrementally rather than as a single, massive transition. 
They created some services in different regions and later found that some of those can be deleted now.

Delete a VPC named nautilus-vpc present in us-east-1 region using Terraform. Make sure to keep the provisioning code, as we might need to provision this instance again later.

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform.

Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.
main.tf
resource "aws_vpc" "this" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"

  tags = {
    Name        = "nautilus-vpc"
  }
}

SOLUTION
Since your resource is named this (i.e., resource "aws_vpc" "this"), the correct target address is aws_vpc.this.

📋 Steps
1. Destroy only the VPC (keeps the code in main.tf):

bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
terraform destroy -target=aws_vpc.this -auto-approve
This will:

Delete the VPC nautilus-vpc in AWS.

Remove it from the Terraform state.

Leave the resource "aws_vpc" "this" { ... } block in main.tf untouched, so you can re-provision it later.

2. Verify the VPC is deleted:

bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-vpcs \
  --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=nautilus-vpc" \
  --query "Vpcs[].VpcId"
Expected output:

json
[]
An empty list confirms the VPC no longer exists.

3. Confirm the provisioning code is still present:

bash
grep -n "nautilus-vpc" main.tf
You should still see your original block:

hcl
resource "aws_vpc" "this" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"

  tags = {
    Name = "nautilus-vpc"
  }
}
4. Confirm the resource is no longer in state:

bash
terraform state list
aws_vpc.this should not appear in the list.

⚠️ If the Destroy Fails Due to Dependencies
AWS automatically creates a default route table, security group, and network ACL with every VPC. These are not separately managed by Terraform in your config, so they won't block the targeted destroy — AWS cleans them up automatically when the VPC is deleted.

However, if you get a DependencyViolation error, it means there are custom resources still inside the VPC (e.g., subnets, internet gateways, custom security groups). Check what exists:

bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-subnets \
  --filters "Name=vpc-id,Values=<vpc-id>" \
  --query "Subnets[].SubnetId"

aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-internet-gateways \
  --filters "Name=attachment.vpc-id,Values=<vpc-id>" \
  --query "InternetGateways[].InternetGatewayId"
If custom resources exist, you'd need to target-destroy them first. But based on the main.tf you shared (VPC only), the single command above should succeed cleanly.

💡 Why -target=aws_vpc.this
Part	Meaning
aws_vpc	The resource type
this	The resource name (the label after the type in your main.tf)
The full address is always <type>.<name> — so in your case, aws_vpc.this.

Once step 2 returns [] and step 3 shows the code is intact, the task is complete.

